StudyData.addIndex(6, {"english":{"syllabus":{"board":"CBSE","match":"textbook","books":["Poorvi"],"note":"Grammar and writing, plus every lesson of Poorvi (summarised in our own words).","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"a-bottle-of-dew","title":"A Bottle of Dew","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A folk tale retold by Sudha Murty. Rama Natha, a landlord’s son, wastes years chasing a magic potion that turns things to gold. A wise sage tells him to collect five litres of dew from banana plants, and in six years of hard work the plantation makes him rich. The sage then explains that the wealth came from labour, not magic.","nTerms":5},{"id":"the-raven-and-the-fox","title":"The Raven and the Fox","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A fable in verse by Jean de La Fontaine. A fox praises a raven who holds food in his beak and asks him to sing. The vain raven opens his beak to croak, drops the food and learns that flattery should be ignored.","nTerms":5},{"id":"rama-to-the-rescue","title":"Rama to the Rescue","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A graphic story, a folk tale from Tamil Nadu. A husband and wife, who cannot sleep, hear a thief break into their house. They pretend to talk about naming their future child ‘Rama’ and keep calling the name aloud. Rama, the village kotwal, hears his name, runs to the house and catches the thief.","nTerms":5},{"id":"the-unlikely-best-friends","title":"The Unlikely Best Friends","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A story about Gajaraj, a lonely royal elephant, and Buntee, a stray dog. They become friends, but when the dog’s owner takes him home both stop eating. The farmer lets the dog go back and the elephant, the dog, the mahout and the farmer end up as friends.","nTerms":5},{"id":"a-friends-prayer","title":"A Friend’s Prayer","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A short poem by Jill Wolf in which the speaker prays that friendship may always be the most important thing in her life. She wishes to be a caring friend, help her friends’ wishes come true and love them just as they are.","nTerms":5},{"id":"the-chair","title":"The Chair","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A grandfather bets his grandson Mario a fruit chaat that many of his many ‘friends’ are only companions. He gives Mario an invisible chair to test them. Only three real friends hold Mario up when he tries to sit, and the others laugh at his falls.","nTerms":5},{"id":"neem-baba","title":"Neem Baba","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A conversation, adapted from S. I. Farooqi, between a girl called Amber and the old neem tree in her courtyard. The tree tells her about its names, its long history and its many uses in medicine, farming and daily life, and asks her to find out more when she grows up.","nTerms":5},{"id":"what-a-bird-thought","title":"What a Bird Thought","section":"Poorvi","summary":"An anonymous poem in which a young bird describes how its idea of the world grows. First the world is a small, round, pale blue shell, then a straw nest, then leaves, and finally it flies beyond the tree and realises it does not know how the world is made.","nTerms":5},{"id":"spices-that-heal-us","title":"Spices that Heal Us","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A letter from Daadi (grandmother) to her grandchildren Vikram and Vaibhavi, who have recovered from an illness. She tells them how common kitchen spices such as turmeric, fenugreek, cumin, asafoetida, cinnamon, clove, ginger, black pepper, fennel, carom and cardamom help with health problems.","nTerms":5},{"id":"change-of-heart","title":"Change of Heart","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A story about Prabhat, who hates losing and plays only games he is sure to win. He cheats to beat a cheerful new boy, Surya, but feels unhappy afterwards. By watching Surya enjoy every game, win or lose, he learns the meaning of sporting spirit.","nTerms":5},{"id":"the-winner","title":"The Winner","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A short poem by Georgia Heard about children playing ball in a neighbour’s field in the evening. They run until they are out of breath and keep playing as dark falls, until night ‘wins’ and the game is over.","nTerms":5},{"id":"yoga-a-way-of-life","title":"Yoga—A Way of Life","section":"Poorvi","summary":"An informative piece about yoga, an ancient Indian practice of the body, mind and spirit. It explains the meaning of the word, the benefits of asanas, breathing and meditation, the WHO mYoga app and International Yoga Day on 21 June.","nTerms":5},{"id":"hamara-bharat-incredible-india","title":"Hamara Bharat—Incredible India!","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A short passage on the unity in diversity of Bharat, followed by ‘Kalakritiyon ka Bharat’, in which children from different states describe a craft of their region: Aipan from Uttarakhand, Dhokra from Odisha, coconut shell craft from Kerala and Kondapalli toys from Andhra Pradesh.","nTerms":5},{"id":"the-kites","title":"The Kites","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A poem by Daphne Lister in which a child watches kites flying like coloured birds and imagines climbing on one, drifting on its paper wings, hearing the wind’s songs and looking down on the park and rooftops while people below stare and wish to be there.","nTerms":5},{"id":"ila-sachani-embroidering-dreams-with-her-feet","title":"Ila Sachani: Embroidering Dreams with her Feet","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A true story of Ila Sachani of Gujarat, born with hands that hung loose by her sides. With help from her family she learnt Kathiawar embroidery using her feet, became famous after state and national exhibitions, and received the President’s Medal.","nTerms":5},{"id":"national-war-memorial","title":"National War Memorial","section":"Poorvi","summary":"A short poem in praise of the National War Memorial in New Delhi, established in February 2019 by the Government of India to honour brave soldiers. It speaks of names etched in stone, an eternal flame and the nation’s pride in its soldiers’ sacrifice.","nTerms":5},{"id":"nouns","title":"Nouns","section":"Grammar","summary":"Nouns are naming words for people, places, animals, things and ideas. This chapter covers the kinds of nouns, how to make plurals, countable and uncountable nouns, and possessive nouns.","nTerms":6},{"id":"pronouns","title":"Pronouns","section":"Grammar","summary":"Pronouns take the place of nouns so that we need not repeat them. This chapter covers personal, possessive, reflexive, demonstrative and interrogative pronouns.","nTerms":6},{"id":"adjectives","title":"Adjectives","section":"Grammar","summary":"Adjectives describe nouns and pronouns. This chapter covers the main kinds of adjectives, the three degrees of comparison, irregular forms, and the use of few and little.","nTerms":6},{"id":"verbs-and-simple-tenses","title":"Verbs and Simple Tenses","section":"Grammar","summary":"Verbs show actions and states, and tenses show the time of the action. This chapter covers verbs, regular and irregular forms, and the simple and continuous forms of the present, past and future tenses.","nTerms":6},{"id":"articles","title":"Articles","section":"Grammar","summary":"The small words a, an and the are called articles. This chapter explains when to use a or an, when to use the, and when no article is needed.","nTerms":6},{"id":"prepositions","title":"Prepositions","section":"Grammar","summary":"Prepositions show how a noun or pronoun is related to another word, such as its place, time or direction. This chapter covers prepositions of place, time and direction, and common fixed pairs.","nTerms":6},{"id":"paragraph-writing","title":"Paragraph Writing","section":"Writing Skills","summary":"A paragraph is a group of sentences about one main idea. This chapter shows how to plan and write a good paragraph with a topic sentence, supporting sentences and a concluding sentence.","nTerms":6},{"id":"informal-letter","title":"Informal Letter","section":"Writing Skills","summary":"An informal letter is a friendly personal letter to a friend or relative. This chapter explains its format, how to plan the body, and the right tone and language.","nTerms":6},{"id":"notice-writing","title":"Notice Writing","section":"Writing Skills","summary":"A notice is a short, clear message put up on a notice board to inform a group of people. This chapter explains the format, the details to include and the style to use.","nTerms":6},{"id":"reading-comprehension","title":"Reading Comprehension","section":"Reading Skills","summary":"Comprehension means understanding what you read. This chapter shows how to read a passage, find the main idea, work out the meanings of difficult words and write good answers.","nTerms":6}]},"englishpb":{"syllabus":{"board":"PSEB","match":"textbook","books":["English (PSEB, Class 6)"],"note":"Follows the PSEB 2026–27 syllabus; lessons summarised in our own words from the PSEB textbook (2023–24 edition).","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"pb-nouns","title":"Nouns","section":"Grammar","summary":"A noun names a person, place, animal, thing or idea. This chapter covers common and proper nouns, singular and plural, gender and collective nouns.","nTerms":5},{"id":"pb-pronouns","title":"Pronouns","section":"Grammar","summary":"A pronoun takes the place of a noun so we do not repeat it. This chapter covers personal, demonstrative and interrogative pronouns.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-verbs-tenses","title":"Verbs, Simple Present and Simple Past","section":"Grammar","summary":"Verbs show action or state. This chapter covers verb forms, the simple present and simple past tenses, and the use of is, am and are.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-articles","title":"Articles","section":"Grammar","summary":"The articles a, an and the come before nouns. This chapter explains when to use each one.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-adjectives-adverbs","title":"Adjectives and Adverbs","section":"Grammar","summary":"Adjectives describe nouns and adverbs describe verbs. This chapter covers their kinds, positions and how adverbs are formed.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-prepositions-conjunctions","title":"Prepositions and Conjunctions","section":"Grammar","summary":"Prepositions show the relation of a noun to another word, and conjunctions join words and sentences.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-punctuation","title":"Punctuation","section":"Grammar","summary":"Punctuation marks and capital letters make writing clear. This chapter shows where to use them.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-word-power","title":"Word Power","section":"Vocabulary","summary":"Animal sounds, antonyms, homophones, one-word substitutions, rhyming words, phrasal verbs and the use of “like”.","nTerms":5},{"id":"pb-letters-applications","title":"Letters and Applications","section":"Writing","summary":"How to write a personal letter to a friend or relative and a formal application to the principal.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-paragraph-picture","title":"Paragraph and Picture Description","section":"Writing","summary":"How to write a clear paragraph on a topic and describe what you see in a picture.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-messages-slogans","title":"Messages and Slogans","section":"Writing","summary":"How to write a short message and a catchy slogan.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-farmhouse-cows","title":"The Farmhouse Cows","section":"Lessons","summary":"Rajinder has moved from a city to a farm and is afraid of cows until his mother shows him how many good things the cows give the family.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-my-furry-friend","title":"My Furry Friend (Poem)","section":"Lessons","summary":"A light-hearted poem by Vandana Lunyal about a pet puppy that grows from a tiny, playful pup into a hungry, frowning, full-grown dog.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-lake-of-the-moon","title":"The Lake of The Moon","section":"Lessons","summary":"During a drought, a herd of elephants tramples a colony of rabbits on the way to a lake. A clever little rabbit tricks the elephant king into leaving the rabbits alone by showing him the Moon in the water.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-giving-tree","title":"The Giving Tree","section":"Lessons","summary":"Shel Silverstein's story of a tree that gives the boy she loves her apples, branches and trunk as he grows up, and in the end offers her stump for him to rest on.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-how-beautiful-rain","title":"How Beautiful is the Rain! (Poem)","section":"Lessons","summary":"A poem by Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, taken from his “Rain in Summer”, welcoming the summer rain that falls after dust and heat.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-peacock-and-crow","title":"The Peacock and the Crow","section":"Lessons","summary":"A crow who dislikes his black feathers covers himself with peacock feathers, is rejected by both peacocks and crows, and learns to accept himself.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-playing-kabaddi","title":"Playing Kabaddi","section":"Lessons","summary":"A short informative passage describing the game of kabaddi, how it is played, and how it has become an international sport.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-writing-a-letter","title":"I am Writing a Letter (Poem)","section":"Lessons","summary":"Margaret G Rhodes's cheerful poem about a child carefully writing a letter to Mummy and posting it.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-kabuliwala","title":"Kabuliwala","section":"Lessons","summary":"Rabindranath Tagore's story of five-year-old Mini and Rehman, a Kabuliwala, whose friendship survives his time in prison and ends with a father's generous gift.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-cracked-pot","title":"The Cracked Pot","section":"Lessons","summary":"A water-bearer in India has two pots, one cracked. After two years the cracked pot says sorry for its flaw, and learns that its crack has watered a path of flowers.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-happy-with-who-i-am","title":"I am Happy with who I am (Poem)","section":"Lessons","summary":"Vandana Lunyal's poem about looking in the mirror and feeling confident, unique and happy with oneself.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-rosy-meets-nessy","title":"Rosy's Meeting with Nessy","section":"Lessons","summary":"Bored on a visit to her aunt in Scotland, Rosy throws stones into Loch Ness and meets Nessy, a gentle plant-eating monster, who gives her a ride.","nTerms":4},{"id":"pb-forgotten-wish","title":"A Forgotten Wish","section":"Lessons","summary":"Bebe needs eye surgery during the Covid-19 flight ban. Her daughter Khwahish takes care of it, and Bebe finally agrees to live with her.","nTerms":4}]},"hindi":{"syllabus":{"board":"CBSE","match":"textbook","books":["Malhar"],"note":"व्याकरण और लेखन, साथ में मल्हार के सभी पाठ (अपने शब्दों में)।","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"varn-vichar","title":"वर्ण विचार (स्वर, व्यंजन, मात्राएँ)","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"भाषा की सबसे छोटी ध्वनि को वर्ण कहते हैं। इस अध्याय में स्वर, व्यंजन और मात्राओं की पहचान सीखेंगे।","nTerms":5},{"id":"sangya","title":"संज्ञा","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"किसी व्यक्ति, वस्तु, स्थान या भाव के नाम को संज्ञा कहते हैं। इसके तीन मुख्य भेद हैं – व्यक्तिवाचक, जातिवाचक और भाववाचक।","nTerms":4},{"id":"sarvanam","title":"सर्वनाम","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"संज्ञा के स्थान पर आने वाले शब्द को सर्वनाम कहते हैं। इससे बार-बार एक ही संज्ञा बोलने से बचा जाता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"ling","title":"लिंग","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"शब्द के जिस रूप से पुरुष या स्त्री जाति का बोध हो, उसे लिंग कहते हैं। हिंदी में दो लिंग होते हैं – पुल्लिंग और स्त्रीलिंग।","nTerms":4},{"id":"vachan","title":"वचन","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"शब्द के जिस रूप से एक या अनेक का बोध हो, उसे वचन कहते हैं। हिंदी में दो वचन हैं – एकवचन और बहुवचन।","nTerms":4},{"id":"vilom-shabd","title":"विलोम शब्द","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"जो शब्द एक-दूसरे का उलटा अर्थ बताते हैं, वे विलोम शब्द कहलाते हैं। इन्हें विपरीतार्थक शब्द भी कहते हैं।","nTerms":4},{"id":"anuchchhed-lekhan","title":"अनुच्छेद लेखन","section":"रचना (लेखन)","summary":"किसी विषय पर कुछ वाक्यों में क्रमबद्ध और सरल रूप से अपने विचार लिखना अनुच्छेद लेखन कहलाता है।","nTerms":4},{"id":"anaupcharik-patra","title":"अनौपचारिक पत्र","section":"रचना (लेखन)","summary":"अपने परिवार, रिश्तेदारों और मित्रों को लिखे जाने वाले पत्र अनौपचारिक पत्र कहलाते हैं। इनमें अपनापन और सरल भाषा होती है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-matribhumi","title":"मातृभूमि","section":"मल्हार","summary":"सोहनलाल द्विवेदी की इस कविता में भारत की प्राकृतिक सुंदरता, पवित्र नदियों, महापुरुषों और गौरवशाली इतिहास का गान है। कवि भारत को अपनी पुण्य-भूमि, कर्म-भूमि, जन्मभूमि और मातृभूमि कहकर उसके प्रति प्रेम प्रकट करते हैं।","nTerms":6},{"id":"malhar-gol","title":"गोल","section":"मल्हार","summary":"यह हॉकी के जादूगर मेजर ध्यानचंद की आत्मकथा का एक अंश है। इसमें वे खेल-भावना की एक घटना सुनाते हैं, अपने सैनिक जीवन और हॉकी से जुड़ाव के बारे में बताते हैं और कहते हैं कि सफलता का राज लगन, साधना और खेल-भावना है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-pahli-boond","title":"पहली बूँद","section":"मल्हार","summary":"गोपालकृष्ण कौल की इस कविता में वर्षा की पहली बूँद के धरती पर गिरने का मनोहारी दृश्य है। पहली बूँद पड़ते ही सूखी धरती में अंकुर फूट पड़ते हैं, घास हरी हो जाती है और बूढ़ी धरती फिर से हरी-भरी होने के लिए ललचा उठती है।","nTerms":6},{"id":"malhar-haar-ki-jeet","title":"हार की जीत","section":"मल्हार","summary":"सुदर्शन की इस कहानी में साधु बाबा भारती अपने प्रिय घोड़े सुलतान से बहुत प्रेम करते हैं। डाकू खड्ग सिंह छल से घोड़ा ले जाता है, पर बाबा की एक प्रार्थना सुनकर उसका हृदय बदल जाता है और वह रात में घोड़ा लौटा देता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-rahim-ke-dohe","title":"रहीम के दोहे","section":"मल्हार","summary":"अब्दुर्रहीम खानखाना के इन सात दोहों में नीति और जीवन के व्यवहार की सीख है – छोटे-बड़े सबका महत्त्व, परोपकार, प्रेम के संबंध की नाज़ुकता, पानी (सम्मान) की रक्षा, संकट में सच्चे मित्र की पहचान और सोच-समझकर बोलना।","nTerms":6},{"id":"malhar-meri-maa","title":"मेरी माँ","section":"मल्हार","summary":"यह क्रांतिकारी रामप्रसाद 'बिस्मिल' की आत्मकथा का अंश है। जेल में लिखे इस अंश में वे अपनी माँ के साहस, सत्य-निष्ठा, शिक्षा के प्रति रुचि और देश-सेवा की प्रेरणा को याद करते हैं और माँ के प्रति कृतज्ञता प्रकट करते हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-jalate-chalo","title":"जलाते चलो","section":"मल्हार","summary":"द्वारिका प्रसाद माहेश्वरी की यह कविता आशा और उत्साह जगाती है। कवि कहते हैं कि स्नेह (तेल और प्रेम) से भरे दीपक जलाते चलो, एक दिन संसार का अँधेरा अवश्य मिटेगा और जब तक एक भी दीप जलता रहेगा, रात को सवेरा मिलेगा।","nTerms":6},{"id":"malhar-satriya-aur-bihu-nritya","title":"सत्रिया और बिहू नृत्य","section":"मल्हार","summary":"लंदन की लड़की एंजेला अपनी माँ के साथ असम आती है, जो वहाँ के नृत्यों पर वृत्तचित्र बना रही हैं। एंजेला बिहू का उत्सव और सत्रिया नृत्य देखती है, असम की लड़की अनु से मित्रता करती है और लौटकर इन नृत्यों को अपनी कक्षा में प्रस्तुत करती है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"malhar-maiya-main-nahin-makhan-khayo","title":"मैया मैं नहिं माखन खायो","section":"मल्हार","summary":"सूरदास के इस पद में बालक श्रीकृष्ण माँ यशोदा से कहते हैं कि उन्होंने माखन नहीं खाया। वे अपनी बात सिद्ध करने के लिए कई तर्क देते हैं और अंत में यशोदा मुसकराकर उन्हें गले से लगा लेती हैं।","nTerms":6},{"id":"malhar-pariksha","title":"परीक्षा","section":"मल्हार","summary":"प्रेमचंद की इस कहानी में देवगढ़ रियासत के बूढ़े दीवान सरदार सुजान सिंह नया दीवान चुनने के लिए अनोखी परीक्षा लेते हैं। हॉकी के बाद एक किसान की फँसी गाड़ी निकालने में घायल होकर भी जो युवक मदद करता है, वही दीवान चुना जाता है।","nTerms":6},{"id":"malhar-chetak-ki-veerta","title":"चेतक की वीरता","section":"मल्हार","summary":"श्यामनारायण पांडेय की यह कविता उनके काव्य 'हल्दीघाटी' का अंश है। इसमें महाराणा प्रताप के घोड़े चेतक की तेज़ गति, बुद्धिमत्ता, चतुराई और रणभूमि में वीरता का ओजस्वी वर्णन है।","nTerms":6},{"id":"malhar-hind-mahasagar-mein-chhota-sa-hindustan","title":"हिंद महासागर में छोटा-सा हिंदुस्तान","section":"मल्हार","summary":"रामधारी सिंह 'दिनकर' के इस यात्रा-वृत्तांत में नैरोबी के राष्ट्रीय उद्यान में शेरों से मुलाकात और मॉरीशस की यात्रा का वर्णन है। लेखक देखते हैं कि वहाँ बसे भारतवंशियों ने अपनी भाषा, संस्कृति और त्योहारों को सँजोकर मॉरीशस को सचमुच छोटा-सा हिंदुस्तान बना दिया है।","nTerms":6},{"id":"malhar-ped-ki-baat","title":"पेड़ की बात","section":"मल्हार","summary":"वैज्ञानिक जगदीशचंद्र बसु के इस निबंध में बीज से अंकुर बनने, जड़ और तने के बढ़ने, पेड़-पौधों के भोजन, प्रकाश की ज़रूरत और फूल-फल की कथा सरल भाषा में बताई गई है। पेड़ मनुष्य की तरह जीवित हैं और संतान (बीज) के लिए अपना सब कुछ अर्पित कर देते हैं।","nTerms":6}]},"hindipb":{"syllabus":{"board":"PSEB","match":"textbook","books":["आओ हिंदी सीखें-6 (द्वितीय भाषा)"],"note":"व्याकरण और लेखन, तथा पुस्तक 'आओ हिंदी सीखें-6' के सभी पाठ (अपने शब्दों में सार)।","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"varn-vichar","title":"वर्ण विचार (स्वर, व्यंजन, मात्राएँ)","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"भाषा की सबसे छोटी ध्वनि को वर्ण कहते हैं। इस अध्याय में स्वर, व्यंजन और मात्राओं की पहचान सीखेंगे।","nTerms":5},{"id":"sangya","title":"संज्ञा","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"किसी व्यक्ति, वस्तु, स्थान या भाव के नाम को संज्ञा कहते हैं। इसके तीन मुख्य भेद हैं – व्यक्तिवाचक, जातिवाचक और भाववाचक।","nTerms":4},{"id":"sarvanam","title":"सर्वनाम","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"संज्ञा के स्थान पर आने वाले शब्द को सर्वनाम कहते हैं। इससे बार-बार एक ही संज्ञा बोलने से बचा जाता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"ling","title":"लिंग","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"शब्द के जिस रूप से पुरुष या स्त्री जाति का बोध हो, उसे लिंग कहते हैं। हिंदी में दो लिंग होते हैं – पुल्लिंग और स्त्रीलिंग।","nTerms":4},{"id":"vachan","title":"वचन","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"शब्द के जिस रूप से एक या अनेक का बोध हो, उसे वचन कहते हैं। हिंदी में दो वचन हैं – एकवचन और बहुवचन।","nTerms":4},{"id":"vilom-shabd","title":"विलोम शब्द","section":"व्याकरण","summary":"जो शब्द एक-दूसरे का उलटा अर्थ बताते हैं, वे विलोम शब्द कहलाते हैं। इन्हें विपरीतार्थक शब्द भी कहते हैं।","nTerms":4},{"id":"anuchchhed-lekhan","title":"अनुच्छेद लेखन","section":"रचना (लेखन)","summary":"किसी विषय पर कुछ वाक्यों में क्रमबद्ध और सरल रूप से अपने विचार लिखना अनुच्छेद लेखन कहलाता है।","nTerms":4},{"id":"anaupcharik-patra","title":"अनौपचारिक पत्र","section":"रचना (लेखन)","summary":"अपने परिवार, रिश्तेदारों और मित्रों को लिखे जाने वाले पत्र अनौपचारिक पत्र कहलाते हैं। इनमें अपनापन और सरल भाषा होती है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"prarthana","title":"प्रार्थना (कविता, संकलित)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस कविता में बच्चे ईश्वर से प्रार्थना करते हैं। वे सबसे प्रेम करना, दुखियों की सहायता करना, माता-पिता और गुरु का आदर करना तथा देश की शान बढ़ाना चाहते हैं। वे देश में हरियाली और खुशहाली की कामना भी करते हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"sabse-bada-dhan","title":"सबसे बड़ा धन (संकलित)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"एक गरीब आदमी राहुल कपिल मुनि से धन कमाने का उपाय पूछता है। मुनि उससे आँखें, हाथ, पैर, मुख आदि के बदले लाखों रुपये देने की बात कहते हैं, पर वह कुछ भी देने को तैयार नहीं होता। तब मुनि समझाते हैं कि स्वस्थ शरीर ही सबसे बड़ा धन है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"jay-jawan-jay-kisan","title":"जय जवान! जय किसान! (कंचन जैन)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"यह पाठ भारत के दूसरे प्रधानमंत्री लाल बहादुर शास्त्री के जीवन पर है। इसमें उनके साहस, सादगी, सिद्धांतों और देश-प्रेम की घटनाएँ बताई गई हैं। उन्होंने 'जय जवान! जय किसान!' का नारा दिया।","nTerms":5},{"id":"indradhanush","title":"इंद्रधनुष (कविता, संकलित)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस कविता में वर्षा के बाद आकाश में निकलने वाले सतरंगे इंद्रधनुष का सुंदर वर्णन है। काले बादल बरसने के बाद सूर्य की किरणें आती हैं और आकाश में रंगों का मेला लग जाता है। कवि बच्चों को भी मुस्कराने और खुशी के रंग बिखेरने के लिए कहता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"imandar-shankar","title":"ईमानदार शंकर (संकलित)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"शंकर नाम का बालक गाँव से नगर काम की तलाश में जाता है। रात को एक हलवाई की दुकान के तख़्ते पर सोने के बाद उसे सुबह पाँच रुपये का नोट मिलता है, जो वह दुकानदार को लौटा देता है। उसकी ईमानदारी से खुश होकर हलवाई उसे काम पर रख लेता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"main-aur-meri-sawari","title":"मैं और मेरी सवारी (सुधा जैन 'सुदीप')","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस पाठ में साइकिल स्वयं अपने बारे में बताती है। वह अपनी उपयोगिता, स्वास्थ्य के लिए अपने लाभ, कम खर्च और प्रदूषण न फैलाने की विशेषता बताती है। अंत में वह बच्चों से कहती है कि मेरी सवारी करो, यातायात के नियम मानो और पर्यावरण बचाओ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"sooraj","title":"सूरज (कविता, डॉ० सुनील बहल)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस कविता में सूरज को पूर्व दिशा से निकलकर उजाला फैलाने वाला बताया गया है। वह बिना छुट्टी लिए रोज़ काम करता है और हमें सुबह जल्दी उठने तथा निरंतर आगे बढ़ने की सीख देता है। वह सौर ऊर्जा का स्रोत भी है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"prayashchit","title":"प्रायश्चित (संकलित)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"होली पर गाँव के शरारती बच्चे गोपू ग्वाले के उपले चुराकर होली में जला देते हैं। दुखी गोपू मुखिया के पास जाता है। मुखिया बच्चों से ही उपले बनवाकर गोपू को लौटाने की सज़ा देते हैं। बच्चे प्रायश्चित करते हैं और सब मिलकर होली खेलते हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"romanchak-kabaddi-mukabala","title":"रोमाँचक कबड्डी मुकाबला (शिव शंकर)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"विद्यालय में सरोजिनी सदन और लक्ष्मीबाई सदन की कबड्डी टीमों के बीच फाइनल मैच होता है। खेल के दाँव-पेंच, रेडर, लोना और बोनस लाइन के साथ रोमांचक मुकाबले का वर्णन है। अंत में सरोजिनी टीम विजयी होती है और खिलाड़ी हाथ मिलाकर एक-दूसरे को बधाई देते हैं।","nTerms":5},{"id":"chidiya-ka-geet","title":"चिड़िया का गीत (कविता, संकलित)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस कविता में एक चिड़िया अपने बचपन से लेकर उड़ना सीखने तक की यात्रा सुनाती है। पहले उसे अंडे के भीतर का स्थान ही संसार लगता था। फिर घोंसला, डालियाँ और अंत में आसमान में उड़ने पर उसे समझ आता है कि संसार बहुत बड़ा है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"doodh-ka-doodh-pani-ka-pani","title":"दूध का दूध, पानी का पानी (संकलित)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"एक न्यायप्रिय राजा की परीक्षा लेने पड़ोसी राजा भेष बदलकर आता है। एक ठग उसका घोड़ा हथियाना चाहता है। राजा अपनी बुद्धि से नौकर, पैसों और घोड़े के तीन झगड़ों का सही फैसला करता है। इसी से 'दूध का दूध, पानी का पानी' मुहावरा बना है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"renuka-jheel","title":"रेणुका झील (डॉ० मीनाक्षी वर्मा)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"यह पाठ एक मित्र के नाम पत्र है। लेखक अपनी हिमाचल प्रदेश के सिरमौर ज़िले में स्थित रेणुका झील की यात्रा का वर्णन करता है। रास्ते में त्रिलोकपुर, नाहन और पाँवटा साहिब भी देखे गए। यात्रा के सुंदर दृश्य, झील, मंदिर और मेले का परिचय है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"kaash-main-bhi","title":"काश! मैं भी (कविता, बिमला देवी)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस कविता में एक कवयित्री की इच्छा है कि वह भी सीमा पर जाकर दुश्मन से लड़े और देश के लिए शहीद हो जाए। वह कारगिल की घाटी का पत्थर बनकर अमर शहीदों को सलाम करना चाहती है। कविता में देशभक्ति और बलिदान का भाव है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"kumari-kalibai","title":"कुमारी काली बाई (श्याम लाल)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"राजस्थान के डूंगरपुर ज़िले के रास्तापाल गाँव की आदिवासी बालिका कालीबाई ने 1947 में अपने अध्यापक सेंगाभाई को बचाने के लिए साहस दिखाया और प्राणों की बलि दे दी। उसकी बहादुरी और देशभक्ति आज भी प्रेरणा देती है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"gurparv","title":"गुरुपर्व (डॉ० सुनील बहल)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस पाठ में गुरु नानक देव जी के प्रकाश पर्व (गुरुपर्व) की तैयारियों और उत्सव का वर्णन है। प्रभातफेरी, गुरुद्वारे की सजावट, नगर कीर्तन, लंगर, अखंड पाठ और दीपमाला जैसी बातों से इस पर्व की खुशी और सेवा-भाव प्रकट होता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"cheenti","title":"चींटी (कविता, संकलित)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस कविता में नन्ही चींटी के परिश्रम, निर्भयता और लगन की प्रशंसा की गई है। चींटी छोटी होकर भी बिना थके काम करती है, गिरकर भी फिर चल पड़ती है। कविता सिखाती है कि मेहनत से हार नहीं माननी चाहिए, श्रम ही फल देता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"pille-bikau-hain","title":"पिल्ले बिकाऊ हैं (संकलित)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"एक दुकानदार ने बोर्ड लगाया था 'पिल्ले बिकाऊ हैं'। एक विकलांग बच्चा सबसे पीछे चलने वाले लंगड़े पिल्ले को खरीदना चाहता है और उसकी पूरी कीमत देने पर अड़ा रहता है। वह कहता है कि इस पिल्ले को ऐसे मालिक की ज़रूरत है जो उसकी मजबूरी समझे। दुकानदार भावुक हो जाता है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"rasoi-ka-taj-sabziyan","title":"रसोई का ताज: सब्जियाँ (एकांकी, डॉ० सुनील बहल)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"यह एक छोटा नाटक है जिसमें नौ बच्चे अलग-अलग सब्ज़ियों के मुखौटे पहनकर अपनी-अपनी खूबियाँ बताते हैं। आलू खुद को सब्ज़ियों का राजा कहता है, पर बाकी सब्ज़ियाँ भी अपने गुण बताती हैं। अंत में मालकिन आकर सबका उपयोग अलग-अलग व्यंजनों में करती है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"ped-lagao","title":"पेड़ लगाओ (कविता, संकलित)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस कविता में कवि सबको अधिक से अधिक पेड़ लगाने की प्रेरणा देता है। वह बताता है कि पेड़ जीवन देने वाले हैं, हरियाली जीवन के लिए ज़रूरी है और पेड़ हमें फल-फूल, सुगंध और स्वच्छ वातावरण देते हैं। पेड़ लगाकर पूरे भारत को हरा-भरा बनाना है।","nTerms":5},{"id":"gyan-ka-bhandar-samachar-patra","title":"ज्ञान का भंडार: समाचार-पत्र (कमलेश भारतीय)","section":"आओ हिंदी सीखें","summary":"इस पाठ में समाचार-पत्र (अख़बार) के महत्त्व का वर्णन है। अख़बार बच्चों, महिलाओं, युवाओं और खिलाड़ियों के लिए जानकारी देता है। स्वतंत्रता आंदोलन में भी समाचार-पत्रों की बड़ी भूमिका रही। समाचार-पत्र पढ़ने से सामान्य ज्ञान बढ़ता है।","nTerms":5}]},"maths":{"syllabus":{"board":"CBSE","match":"textbook","books":["Ganita Prakash"],"reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"patterns-in-mathematics","title":"Patterns in Mathematics","section":"Patterns and Algebra","summary":"Numbers and shapes follow patterns. In this chapter we look for the rule behind a sequence, meet triangular, square and cube numbers and the Virahanka–Fibonacci numbers, and see how different patterns are linked.","nTerms":6},{"id":"lines-and-angles","title":"Lines and Angles","section":"Geometry","summary":"We learn the difference between a point, a line segment, a ray and a line, see how lines can be intersecting, parallel or perpendicular, and learn to name, measure and classify angles.","nTerms":6},{"id":"number-play","title":"Number Play","section":"Number","summary":"We play with numbers to find out how they behave: odd and even sums, clever ways of adding, palindromes, and famous number games such as the Collatz sequence and Kaprekar's 6174.","nTerms":6},{"id":"data-handling-and-presentation","title":"Data Handling and Presentation","section":"Data","summary":"We collect information, organise it with tally marks and frequency tables, show it as pictographs and bar graphs, and find simple summaries such as the range and the mean (average).","nTerms":6},{"id":"prime-time","title":"Prime Time","section":"Number","summary":"We study factors and multiples, find out what makes a number prime or composite, learn the divisibility tests, break numbers into prime factors and find common factors and multiples.","nTerms":6},{"id":"perimeter-and-area","title":"Perimeter and Area","section":"Measurement","summary":"Perimeter is the distance around a shape and area is the surface it covers. We find both for rectangles and squares, find the area of triangles, and use them in everyday problems like fencing and tiling.","nTerms":6},{"id":"fractions","title":"Fractions","section":"Number","summary":"A fraction shows equal parts of a whole or of a collection. We learn the types of fractions, equivalent fractions, how to compare them and how to add and subtract them.","nTerms":6},{"id":"playing-with-constructions","title":"Playing with Constructions","section":"Geometry","summary":"Using only a ruler and a compass we draw circles, copy line segments, draw perpendicular bisectors, construct angles such as 60°, 30° and 90°, and build rectangles, squares and triangles.","nTerms":6},{"id":"symmetry","title":"Symmetry","section":"Geometry","summary":"A shape is symmetric when one half is the mirror image of the other, or when it looks the same after a turn. We find lines of symmetry in shapes and letters, study mirror images and look at rotational symmetry.","nTerms":6},{"id":"the-other-side-of-zero","title":"The Other Side of Zero","section":"Number","summary":"Negative numbers help us describe temperatures below zero, debts and depths. We study integers, place them on the number line, compare them, and add and subtract them.","nTerms":5}]},"mathspb":{"syllabus":{"board":"PSEB","match":"textbook","books":["Mathematics (earlier NCERT book)"],"note":"PSEB 2026–27 uses the earlier NCERT book for this class; chapters follow its list.","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"knowing-our-numbers","title":"Knowing Our Numbers","section":"Number","summary":"This chapter revises large numbers, the Indian and international place-value systems, comparing, estimating and rounding numbers, and reading Roman numerals.","nTerms":5},{"id":"whole-numbers","title":"Whole Numbers","section":"Number","summary":"Whole numbers are 0, 1, 2, 3, … This chapter studies the number line, the properties of the four operations on whole numbers and some patterns.","nTerms":5},{"id":"playing-with-numbers","title":"Playing with Numbers","section":"Number","summary":"This chapter studies factors and multiples, prime and composite numbers, tests of divisibility, prime factorisation, and finding the HCF and LCM.","nTerms":6},{"id":"basic-geometrical-ideas","title":"Basic Geometrical Ideas","section":"Geometry","summary":"This chapter introduces points, lines, segments and rays, then curves, polygons, angles, triangles, quadrilaterals and circles with their parts.","nTerms":6},{"id":"understanding-elementary-shapes","title":"Understanding Elementary Shapes","section":"Geometry","summary":"This chapter covers measuring line segments, the types of angles and how to measure them, perpendicular lines, kinds of triangles and quadrilaterals, and simple 3D shapes.","nTerms":6},{"id":"integers","title":"Integers","section":"Number","summary":"Integers include negative numbers, zero and positive numbers. This chapter shows how to place them on a number line, compare them, and add and subtract them.","nTerms":4},{"id":"fractions","title":"Fractions","section":"Number","summary":"This chapter covers fractions as parts of a whole, proper, improper and mixed fractions, equivalent fractions, comparing them and adding and subtracting fractions.","nTerms":6},{"id":"decimals","title":"Decimals","section":"Number","summary":"Decimals extend place value to tenths, hundredths and thousandths. This chapter covers reading, comparing, converting and adding and subtracting decimals, with money, length and mass.","nTerms":5},{"id":"data-handling","title":"Data Handling","section":"Data","summary":"This chapter shows how to collect and organise data using tally marks and frequency tables, and how to read and draw pictographs and bar graphs.","nTerms":5},{"id":"mensuration","title":"Mensuration","section":"Mensuration","summary":"This chapter introduces perimeter as the distance round a closed figure and area as the space it covers, with formulas for rectangles and squares.","nTerms":4},{"id":"algebra","title":"Algebra","section":"Algebra","summary":"Algebra uses letters for unknown or changing numbers. This chapter covers variables, patterns written as rules, expressions and solving simple equations.","nTerms":4},{"id":"ratio-and-proportion","title":"Ratio and Proportion","section":"Number","summary":"A ratio compares two quantities of the same kind by division. This chapter covers simplifying ratios, equivalent ratios, proportion and the unitary method.","nTerms":5},{"id":"symmetry","title":"Symmetry","section":"Geometry","summary":"A figure has line symmetry when it can be folded along a line so that the two halves match exactly. This chapter finds lines of symmetry and looks at reflection.","nTerms":4},{"id":"practical-geometry","title":"Practical Geometry","section":"Geometry","summary":"This chapter teaches accurate constructions with a ruler, compass, set squares and protractor: circles, line segments, perpendiculars, bisectors and angles.","nTerms":5}]},"punjabi":{"syllabus":{"board":"CBSE","match":"topic","books":[],"note":"Grammar and writing skills for CBSE and PSEB students. Textbook lessons are not included.","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"gurmukhi-duhrai","title":"ਗੁਰਮੁਖੀ ਦੁਹਰਾਈ","section":"ਵਿਆਕਰਨ","summary":"ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਪੈਂਤੀ ਅੱਖਰੀ, ਲਗਾਂ-ਮਾਤਰਾਂ ਅਤੇ ਲਗਾਖਰਾਂ ਦੀ ਦੁਹਰਾਈ ਕੀਤੀ ਗਈ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"naanv","title":"ਨਾਂਵ","section":"ਵਿਆਕਰਨ","summary":"ਕਿਸੇ ਵਿਅਕਤੀ, ਥਾਂ, ਵਸਤੂ ਜਾਂ ਭਾਵ ਦੇ ਨਾਂ ਨੂੰ ਨਾਂਵ ਕਹਿੰਦੇ ਹਨ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਨਾਂਵ ਦੀਆਂ ਪੰਜ ਕਿਸਮਾਂ ਸਮਝਾਈਆਂ ਗਈਆਂ ਹਨ।","nTerms":6},{"id":"padnaanv","title":"ਪੜਨਾਂਵ","section":"ਵਿਆਕਰਨ","summary":"ਨਾਂਵ ਦੀ ਥਾਂ ਵਰਤੇ ਜਾਣ ਵਾਲੇ ਸ਼ਬਦਾਂ ਨੂੰ ਪੜਨਾਂਵ ਕਹਿੰਦੇ ਹਨ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਪੜਨਾਂਵ ਦੀ ਪਛਾਣ ਅਤੇ ਮੁੱਖ ਕਿਸਮਾਂ ਦਿੱਤੀਆਂ ਗਈਆਂ ਹਨ।","nTerms":5},{"id":"ling","title":"ਲਿੰਗ","section":"ਵਿਆਕਰਨ","summary":"ਸ਼ਬਦ ਦੇ ਨਰ ਜਾਂ ਮਦੀਨ ਹੋਣ ਦੀ ਪਛਾਣ ਲਿੰਗ ਤੋਂ ਹੁੰਦੀ ਹੈ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਪੁਲਿੰਗ ਤੋਂ ਇਸਤਰੀ ਲਿੰਗ ਬਣਾਉਣ ਦੀਆਂ ਸਧਾਰਨ ਵਿਧੀਆਂ ਦਿੱਤੀਆਂ ਗਈਆਂ ਹਨ।","nTerms":4},{"id":"vachan","title":"ਵਚਨ","section":"ਵਿਆਕਰਨ","summary":"ਸ਼ਬਦ ਦੇ ਇੱਕ ਜਾਂ ਇੱਕ ਤੋਂ ਵੱਧ ਹੋਣ ਦਾ ਪਤਾ ਵਚਨ ਤੋਂ ਲੱਗਦਾ ਹੈ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਇੱਕ ਵਚਨ ਤੋਂ ਬਹੁ ਵਚਨ ਬਣਾਉਣ ਦੇ ਨਿਯਮ ਦਿੱਤੇ ਗਏ ਹਨ।","nTerms":4},{"id":"virodhi-shabad","title":"ਵਿਰੋਧੀ ਸ਼ਬਦ","section":"ਵਿਆਕਰਨ","summary":"ਉਲਟ ਅਰਥ ਵਾਲੇ ਸ਼ਬਦਾਂ ਨੂੰ ਵਿਰੋਧੀ ਸ਼ਬਦ ਕਹਿੰਦੇ ਹਨ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਰੋਜ਼ਾਨਾ ਵਰਤੋਂ ਦੇ ਵਿਰੋਧੀ ਸ਼ਬਦਾਂ ਦੇ ਜੋੜੇ ਦਿੱਤੇ ਗਏ ਹਨ।","nTerms":4},{"id":"paira-rachna","title":"ਪੈਰਾ ਰਚਨਾ","section":"ਲੇਖ ਰਚਨਾ","summary":"ਕਿਸੇ ਇੱਕ ਵਿਸ਼ੇ ਬਾਰੇ ਕੁਝ ਵਾਕਾਂ ਵਿੱਚ ਆਪਣੇ ਵਿਚਾਰ ਲਿਖਣ ਨੂੰ ਪੈਰਾ ਰਚਨਾ ਕਹਿੰਦੇ ਹਨ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਚੰਗਾ ਪੈਰਾ ਲਿਖਣ ਦੇ ਢੰਗ ਸਮਝਾਏ ਗਏ ਹਨ।","nTerms":4},{"id":"nijji-chitthi","title":"ਨਿੱਜੀ ਚਿੱਠੀ","section":"ਲੇਖ ਰਚਨਾ","summary":"ਆਪਣੇ ਪਰਿਵਾਰ ਜਾਂ ਦੋਸਤ ਨੂੰ ਲਿਖੀ ਜਾਂਦੀ ਚਿੱਠੀ ਨੂੰ ਨਿੱਜੀ ਚਿੱਠੀ ਕਹਿੰਦੇ ਹਨ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਚਿੱਠੀ ਦੇ ਭਾਗ ਅਤੇ ਲਿਖਣ ਦਾ ਢੰਗ ਸਮਝਾਇਆ ਗਿਆ ਹੈ।","nTerms":4},{"id":"kahani-rachna","title":"ਕਹਾਣੀ ਰਚਨਾ","section":"ਲੇਖ ਰਚਨਾ","summary":"ਸੰਕੇਤਾਂ ਜਾਂ ਸਿਰਲੇਖ ਦੀ ਮਦਦ ਨਾਲ ਆਪਣੇ ਸ਼ਬਦਾਂ ਵਿੱਚ ਕਹਾਣੀ ਲਿਖਣੀ ਕਹਾਣੀ ਰਚਨਾ ਹੈ। ਇਸ ਪਾਠ ਵਿੱਚ ਕਹਾਣੀ ਦੇ ਭਾਗ ਅਤੇ ਲਿਖਣ ਦਾ ਢੰਗ ਦਿੱਤਾ ਗਿਆ ਹੈ।","nTerms":5}]},"science":{"syllabus":{"board":"CBSE","match":"textbook","books":["Curiosity"],"reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"the-wonderful-world-of-science","title":"The Wonderful World of Science","section":"Introduction","summary":"Science is the careful study of nature through curiosity, questions, observation and experiments. This chapter introduces the scientific method, the main branches of science and the habits that make a good scientist.","nTerms":6},{"id":"diversity-in-the-living-world","title":"Diversity in the Living World","section":"Biology","summary":"Earth is home to millions of kinds of plants and animals. This chapter looks at how living things are grouped by their features, how plants differ in their stems, leaves and roots, and how organisms are adapted to the places where they live.","nTerms":6},{"id":"mindful-eating-a-path-to-a-healthy-body","title":"Mindful Eating: A Path to a Healthy Body","section":"Biology","summary":"Food gives us energy and the materials to grow and stay healthy. This chapter explains where food comes from, the nutrients in it, deficiency diseases, and how to eat mindfully: a balanced, varied and safe diet in the right amount.","nTerms":6},{"id":"exploring-magnets","title":"Exploring Magnets","section":"Physics","summary":"Magnets attract certain materials, have two poles and point north-south when free to turn. This chapter covers magnetic and non-magnetic materials, poles, attraction and repulsion, the compass, and how to use and care for magnets.","nTerms":6},{"id":"measurement-of-length-and-motion","title":"Measurement of Length and Motion","section":"Physics","summary":"To compare lengths we need standard units. This chapter covers units of length and how to measure straight and curved lengths accurately, and then the different types of motion and the idea that motion depends on a reference point.","nTerms":6},{"id":"materials-around-us","title":"Materials Around Us","section":"Chemistry","summary":"Everything around us is made of materials. This chapter shows how we group materials by properties such as appearance, hardness, solubility, transparency and floating or sinking, and how we choose the right material for each use.","nTerms":6},{"id":"temperature-and-its-measurement","title":"Temperature and its Measurement","section":"Physics","summary":"Temperature tells us how hot or cold something is. This chapter shows why our sense of touch is unreliable, how thermometers work and are used safely, and how heat flows from hotter to colder objects.","nTerms":6},{"id":"a-journey-through-states-of-water","title":"A Journey through States of Water","section":"Chemistry","summary":"Water exists as solid ice, liquid water and gaseous water vapour, and keeps changing between them. This chapter follows water through melting, freezing, evaporation, condensation and the water cycle, and shows why saving water matters.","nTerms":6},{"id":"methods-of-separation-in-everyday-life","title":"Methods of Separation in Everyday Life","section":"Chemistry","summary":"Most things we use are mixtures. This chapter explains why we separate mixtures and the methods used, such as handpicking, winnowing, threshing, sieving, sedimentation, decantation, filtration, evaporation and condensation, with everyday and farm examples.","nTerms":6},{"id":"living-creatures-exploring-their-characteristics","title":"Living Creatures: Exploring their Characteristics","section":"Biology","summary":"Living things share common characteristics: they need food, respire, respond to their surroundings, grow, reproduce, remove waste and move in some way. This chapter explains these characteristics in plants and animals and how living things differ from non-living things.","nTerms":6},{"id":"natures-treasures","title":"Nature's Treasures","section":"Earth and Space","summary":"Air, water, soil, forests, minerals and fuels are natural resources that we use every day. This chapter explains what they are, how renewable and non-renewable resources differ, and why we must use them wisely and conserve them.","nTerms":6},{"id":"beyond-earth","title":"Beyond Earth","section":"Earth and Space","summary":"Look up at the sky and you see the Sun, Moon, stars and planets. This chapter explains how these objects differ, why the Moon seems to change shape, how stars form patterns called constellations, and how the Sun and its eight planets form our Solar System.","nTerms":6}]},"sciencepb":{"syllabus":{"board":"PSEB","match":"textbook","books":["Science (earlier NCERT book)"],"note":"PSEB 2026–27 uses the earlier NCERT book for this class; chapters follow its list.","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"components-of-food","title":"Components of Food","section":"Biology","summary":"Our food contains nutrients that give energy, help us grow and keep us healthy. This chapter names the main nutrients, tells how to test for them, and explains deficiency diseases and a balanced diet.","nTerms":6},{"id":"sorting-materials-into-groups","title":"Sorting Materials into Groups","section":"Chemistry","summary":"Objects are made of different materials. We group materials by their properties such as appearance, hardness, solubility in water, floating or sinking, and how much light passes through them.","nTerms":5},{"id":"separation-of-substances","title":"Separation of Substances","section":"Chemistry","summary":"Most things we use are mixtures of two or more substances. Useful and harmful parts must often be separated. This chapter describes the common methods of separation and when each one is used.","nTerms":6},{"id":"getting-to-know-plants","title":"Getting to Know Plants","section":"Biology","summary":"Plants can be grouped as herbs, shrubs and trees. This chapter looks at their parts, namely the root, stem, leaf and flower, and what each does for the plant.","nTerms":6},{"id":"body-movements","title":"Body Movements","section":"Biology","summary":"Our bones, muscles and joints work together to let us move. This chapter describes the human skeleton, kinds of joints and how animals such as fish, birds, snakes and earthworms move.","nTerms":5},{"id":"living-organisms-and-their-surroundings","title":"The Living Organisms and their Surroundings","section":"Biology","summary":"Living things live in particular surroundings called habitats and have features that help them survive there. This chapter covers habitats, adaptations and the characteristics that distinguish living things from non-living things.","nTerms":5},{"id":"motion-and-measurement-of-distances","title":"Motion and Measurement of Distances","section":"Physics","summary":"Measurement means comparing a quantity with a standard unit. This chapter introduces the SI unit of length, how to measure lengths correctly, and the different kinds of motion.","nTerms":6},{"id":"light-shadows-and-reflections","title":"Light, Shadows and Reflections","section":"Physics","summary":"Light helps us see objects. This chapter classifies objects by how they treat light, explains how shadows form, how a pinhole camera works, and how mirrors reflect light.","nTerms":5},{"id":"electricity-and-circuits","title":"Electricity and Circuits","section":"Physics","summary":"An electric cell supplies electricity that can light a bulb when connected in a closed path. This chapter describes the parts of a bulb and cell, a simple circuit and switch, and the difference between conductors and insulators.","nTerms":6},{"id":"fun-with-magnets","title":"Fun with Magnets","section":"Physics","summary":"Magnets attract some materials and have poles that point north and south. This chapter covers magnetic and non-magnetic materials, poles, attraction and repulsion, the compass and the care of magnets.","nTerms":5},{"id":"air-around-us","title":"Air Around Us","section":"Chemistry","summary":"Air surrounds us and is vital to living things. This chapter describes what air contains, the role of oxygen, how plants and animals use air, and the idea that moving air is wind.","nTerms":5}]},"sst":{"syllabus":{"board":"CBSE","match":"textbook","books":["Exploring Society: India and Beyond"],"reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"locating-places-on-earth","title":"Locating Places on the Earth","section":"India and the World: Land and the People","summary":"How latitudes, longitudes and the grid help us find any place on the Earth, why time changes from place to place, and how India's position is described.","nTerms":5},{"id":"oceans-and-continents","title":"Oceans and Continents","section":"India and the World: Land and the People","summary":"The Earth's surface is divided into seven large landmasses called continents and five oceans, and the continents have slowly moved over millions of years.","nTerms":5},{"id":"landforms-and-life","title":"Landforms and Life","section":"India and the World: Land and the People","summary":"Mountains, plateaus and plains give a place its own climate, soil and vegetation, and these in turn shape how people live, work and build their homes.","nTerms":5},{"id":"timeline-and-sources-of-history","title":"Timeline and Sources of History","section":"Tapestry of the Past","summary":"How historians arrange events on a timeline using BCE and CE, and how they use archaeological, written and oral sources to reconstruct the past.","nTerms":5},{"id":"india-that-is-bharat","title":"India, That Is Bharat","section":"Tapestry of the Past","summary":"How the land we call India got its names, what its physical features and neighbours are, and how the Constitution describes the country as 'India, that is Bharat'.","nTerms":4},{"id":"beginnings-of-indian-civilisation","title":"The Beginnings of Indian Civilisation","section":"Tapestry of the Past","summary":"From the early hunter-gatherers of the Stone Age to the planned cities of the Harappan civilisation, and how people learned farming and settled in villages.","nTerms":5},{"id":"indias-cultural-roots","title":"India's Cultural Roots","section":"Our Cultural Heritage and Knowledge Traditions","summary":"A look at some of India's oldest knowledge traditions, including ancient texts, philosophies, science, music, dance and architecture, which still shape life in the country today.","nTerms":5},{"id":"unity-in-diversity","title":"Unity in Diversity, or 'Many in the One'","section":"Our Cultural Heritage and Knowledge Traditions","summary":"India has many languages, religions, dresses, foods and festivals, yet its people are bound together by shared history, shared values and the Constitution. This is the idea of 'many in the One'.","nTerms":5},{"id":"family-and-community","title":"Family and Community","section":"Governance and Democracy","summary":"The family is the first unit of society where we learn care and values, and a community is a group that shares a place, culture or way of life. Every person has rights and responsibilities in both.","nTerms":5},{"id":"grassroots-democracy-part-1-governance","title":"Grassroots Democracy – Part 1: Governance","section":"Governance and Democracy","summary":"Why democracy needs governments at several levels, how power is shared between the Union, state and local governments, and how the Gram Sabha and Panchayati Raj bring democracy to the grassroots.","nTerms":5},{"id":"grassroots-democracy-part-2-local-government-rural-areas","title":"Grassroots Democracy – Part 2: Local Government in Rural Areas","section":"Governance and Democracy","summary":"How the Gram Panchayat, Panchayat Samiti and Zila Parishad are elected and what they do for villages, and how reserved seats make sure women and weaker sections also take part.","nTerms":5},{"id":"grassroots-democracy-part-3-local-government-urban-areas","title":"Grassroots Democracy – Part 3: Local Government in Urban Areas","section":"Governance and Democracy","summary":"How towns and cities are governed by urban local bodies, how councillors are elected from wards, and which services these bodies provide to city people.","nTerms":5},{"id":"the-value-of-work","title":"The Value of Work","section":"Economic Life Around Us","summary":"What work means, why all honest work adds value to society, and how people earn a living through paid jobs as well as unpaid household work.","nTerms":5},{"id":"economic-activities-around-us","title":"Economic Activities Around Us","section":"Economic Life Around Us","summary":"How people earn a living through primary, secondary and tertiary activities, and how these three kinds of activity depend on one another.","nTerms":5}]},"sstpb":{"syllabus":{"board":"PSEB","match":"textbook","books":["Social Science (PSEB, Class 6)"],"note":"Follows the PSEB 2026–27 syllabus; written from the syllabus topics, not the PSEB book's own text.","reviewed":false},"chapters":[{"id":"earth-solar-system","title":"Earth – a member of the Solar System","section":"Geography","summary":"The Sun, the planets, the Moon and other heavenly bodies, and why the Earth is the only planet known to support life.","nTerms":5},{"id":"motions-of-the-earth","title":"Motions of the Earth","section":"Geography","summary":"How the Earth's rotation causes day and night and its revolution, with the tilted axis, causes the seasons.","nTerms":5},{"id":"globe-model-of-earth","title":"Globe – the Model of the Earth","section":"Geography","summary":"How a globe represents the Earth, and how latitudes, longitudes and the Prime Meridian help us find places and time.","nTerms":5},{"id":"how-maps-help-us","title":"How Maps Help Us","section":"Geography","summary":"What a map is, the kinds of maps, and how to read one using direction, scale and symbols.","nTerms":5},{"id":"realms-of-the-earth","title":"Realms of the Earth","section":"Geography","summary":"The four realms of the Earth: lithosphere, hydrosphere, atmosphere and biosphere, with the continents and oceans.","nTerms":5},{"id":"our-india-in-the-world","title":"Our India – in the World","section":"Geography","summary":"Where India lies in the world, its size, neighbours, states and physical divisions.","nTerms":5},{"id":"study-of-ancient-history-sources","title":"Study of Ancient History – Sources","section":"History","summary":"How historians learn about the past from archaeological and literary sources, and how time is counted as BCE and CE.","nTerms":5},{"id":"early-man-stone-age","title":"Early Man – the Stone Age","section":"History","summary":"How early humans lived in the Old, Middle and New Stone Ages, and how they discovered fire, farming and the wheel.","nTerms":5},{"id":"harappan-civilization","title":"The Harappan Civilization (3000–1900 BCE)","section":"History","summary":"India's earliest cities in the Indus valley: their planning, houses, trade, crafts, and what we do not know about them.","nTerms":5},{"id":"the-vedic-age","title":"The Vedic Age (1500–500 BCE)","section":"History","summary":"The Early and Later Vedic periods: the Vedas, the way of life, society and rulers.","nTerms":5},{"id":"india-600-to-400-bce","title":"India from 600 BCE to 400 BCE","section":"History","summary":"The sixteen Mahajanapadas, the rise of Magadha, and the teachings of Mahavira and Gautama Buddha.","nTerms":5},{"id":"mauryas-and-shungas","title":"The Mauryas and Shungas (300–200 BCE)","section":"History","summary":"The Mauryan Empire founded by Chandragupta, Ashoka and his Dhamma, and the Shunga rulers who followed.","nTerms":5},{"id":"india-200-bce-to-300-ce","title":"India from 200 BCE to 300 CE","section":"History","summary":"Foreign rulers in the north-west, the Kushanas, the Satavahanas in the Deccan, and the Sangam age in the far south.","nTerms":5},{"id":"gupta-empire","title":"The Gupta Empire (300–600 CE)","section":"History","summary":"The rise of the Guptas, their great rulers, and the achievements in science, art and literature of the Gupta age.","nTerms":5},{"id":"period-of-harshavardhana","title":"The Period of Harshavardhana (600–650 CE)","section":"History","summary":"King Harsha of Thanesar and Kannauj, his rule, his patronage of learning and the account of the Chinese traveller Xuanzang.","nTerms":5},{"id":"chalukyas-and-pallavas","title":"The Chalukyas and Pallavas","section":"History","summary":"Two great southern dynasties of the 6th–8th centuries CE: the Chalukyas of Badami and the Pallavas of Kanchipuram, and their art.","nTerms":5},{"id":"india-and-the-world","title":"India and the World","section":"History","summary":"India's contacts with other lands in ancient times through trade, the spread of Buddhism, and the exchange of ideas.","nTerms":5},{"id":"community-meets-human-needs","title":"Community Meets Human Needs","section":"Civics","summary":"What a community is, how members cooperate to meet needs, and the institutions and services that serve them.","nTerms":5},{"id":"rural-development-and-local-government","title":"Rural Development and Local Government","section":"Civics","summary":"How villages are governed through the Panchayati Raj system and how it works for rural development.","nTerms":5},{"id":"urban-development-and-local-government","title":"Urban Development and Local Government","section":"Civics","summary":"How towns and cities are governed by municipal bodies and the services they provide.","nTerms":5},{"id":"preservation-of-public-property","title":"Preservation of Public Property","section":"Civics","summary":"What public property is, why it belongs to everyone, and how citizens must protect it.","nTerms":5}]}});
